\subsection{Traffic Efficiency Metrics}
The simulator logs per-vehicle trajectories and computes:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Average and maximum waiting time (s)}:
    \[
    \overline{W}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|}\sum_{v\in\mathcal{V}} W_v,
    \qquad
    W_{\max}=\max_{v\in\mathcal{V}} W_v
    \]
    \item \textbf{Average speed (m/s)}:
    \[
    \overline{V}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{S}|}\sum_{(v,t)\in\mathcal{S}} V_{v,t}
    \]
    \item \textbf{Throughput (arrived vehicles)} and \textbf{throughput per hour}:
    \[
    \text{Throughput}_{/h}=\frac{N_{\text{arrived}}\cdot 3600}{T_{\text{sim}}}
    \]
    \item \textbf{Congestion index}: proportion of lane-time samples with queueing, using \(W_{v,t}>5s\) as a queueing indicator.
    \item \textbf{Average Queue Length (AQL)} and \textbf{pressure proxy}: computed from queued-vehicle counts over time, including bus-weighting when vehicles are labeled as buses.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Environmental and Infrastructure Metrics}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Emissions and fuel}: SUMO emission outputs provide total \(CO_2\), \(NO_x\), \(PM_x\), and fuel consumption. For OSM maps, emissions are collected using SUMO’s native emission-output (to avoid expensive per-vehicle TraCI emission calls).
    \item \textbf{Lane occupancy}: if induction-loop detectors are present, mean occupancy and its dispersion are computed to characterize road utilization.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Statistical Analysis (Academic Experiments)}
For multiple independent runs, summary statistics are reported using mean, standard deviation, and a 95\% confidence interval (t-distribution):
\[
\mathrm{CI}_{95\%}=\bar{x}\pm t_{0.975,\,n-1}\cdot\frac{s}{\sqrt{n}}
\]
and pairwise comparisons are assessed using both an independent t-test and a Mann--Whitney U test. In addition to reporting statistical significance, the results section interprets effect direction and practical relevance (e.g., absolute seconds saved per vehicle, throughput change, and emission reductions).

\section{Visualization Dashboard}
The system provides a Flask-based web dashboard for configuring experiments and reviewing results. The dashboard workflow is:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The user selects dataset, strategy, and simulation duration.
    \item A SUMO-backed simulation run is launched (GUI or headless).
    \item After completion, raw logs (CSV) and aggregated metrics (JSON) are stored.
    \item The interface displays KPI summaries and comparison plots (e.g., waiting time, throughput, congestion), and supports multi-run academic experiments for statistical summaries.
\end{itemize}

